   "summary": "The organisers of the 6th Inter Tourism Expo Accra (INTTA) 2026 have officially released the conference agenda, outlining three days of high-level discussions, investment presentations, policy engagements and business networking aimed at positioning Ghana as a competitive 24-hour investment economy.",
   "summary_zh": "第六届阿克拉国际旅游博览会（INTTA）2026年的组织者已正式发布会议议程，概述了为期三天的高级别讨论、投资推介、政策互动和商务 networking，旨在将加纳定位为具有竞争力的24小时投资经济体。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The organisers of the 6th Inter Tourism Expo Accra (INTTA) 2026 have officially released the conference agenda, outlining three days of high-level discussions, investment presentations, policy engagements and business networking aimed at positioning Ghana as a competitive 24-hour investment economy. The release of the agenda marks a significant step towards bringing public- and private-sector stakeholders together to identify investment opportunities, strengthen institutional partnerships and translate discussions into commercially viable initiatives. Three days of policy dialogue, investment and enterprise development The opening programme will also include diplomatic and diasporan remarks, cultural performances, a keynote address, a masterclass on tourism as infrastructure investment, and a Public-Private Partnerships Forum examining how institutional collaboration can advance Ghana’s 24-Hour Economy agenda. Day Two, Thursday, 22 October, will focus on Investment Marketplace and Deal Flow , with the theme “From Vision to Transactions.” Discussions will address modular energy solutions for tourism and hospitality, digital infrastructure and fintech, hospitality real estate, agribusiness, agro-processing, agrotourism, ecotourism and health tourism. A major feature of the day will be the Investment Pitch Sessions, which are designed to connect investment-ready companies and project sponsors with potential investors and relevant financial and institutional partners. Participating enterprises will present opportunities across the designated investment tracks, creating a platform for investor engagement, due diligence discussions and potential partnerships. Day Three, Friday, 23 October, will examine 24-Hour Economy Policy and Youth-Led Entrepreneurship , with particular attention to the role of tourism in round-the-clock economic activity. The programme will feature discussions on small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), artificial intelligence as a tool for transforming youth-led side businesses into scalable enterprises, and the role of tourism in Ghana’s economic transformation. A policy keynote address by Augustus Obuadum Tanoh, Presidential Adviser for Ghana’s 24-Hour Economy Authority, is scheduled, alongside a fireside chat on positioning tourism as a driver of Ghana’s round-the-clock transformation. The Youth Creative Changemakers Innovation Zone will provide a platform for selected youth-led ventures and creative initiatives working across tourism, technology, trade, agribusiness and the creative economy to present their ideas to conference delegates, investors and industry leaders. The programme will conclude with a closing plenary on commitments, deal flow and next steps, intended to identify practical follow-up actions and opportunities for collaboration arising from the three-day conference. Commenting on the release of the agenda today, Emmanuel Treku, Executive Chairman and Convener of INTTA 2026 , said the Expo is designed to go beyond conventional conference discussions by creating practical connections between investment opportunities, enterprise development and strategic partnerships. “The release of the 2026 INTTA agenda is an important milestone as we prepare to bring together the people, institutions, ideas and capital that can help unlock Ghana’s tourism and wider economic potential. “Our ambition is to position tourism as a strategic driver of investment, job creation, enterprise growth and regional development. Tourism does not operate in isolation. It connects hospitality, transport, agriculture, technology, real estate, culture, financial services and international trade. When these sectors work together, the opportunities for sustainable economic growth become much greater. “This year’s programme is deliberately structured to encourage meaningful dialogue, showcase investment opportunities and create a pathway from ideas to bankable partnerships.",
