   "published": "Sun, 18 Oct 2026 04:00:00 GMT",
   "_dt": 1792296000.0,
   "ageDays": 1.22,
   "summary": "At the close of the IMF/World Bank Annual Meetings in Bangkok, Thailand, Central Bank of Nigeria Governor Olayemi Cardoso discusses Nigeria’s economic recovery reforms to attract long-term investment and the monetary policy implications of easing inflation. Headline inflation declined to 14.63 per cent in September 2026, a development we welcomed during our stay here, from approximately 34.8 per cent at the end of 2024.",
   "summary_zh": "在泰国曼谷举行的国际货币基金组织/世界银行年度会议结束时，尼日利亚中央银行行长Olayemi Cardoso讨论了尼日利亚的经济复苏改革以吸引长期投资以及缓解通胀的货币政策影响。标题通胀率在2026年9月降至14.63%，这是我们在逗留期间欢迎的发展，此前2024年底约为34.8%。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "At the close of the IMF/World Bank Annual Meetings in Bangkok, Thailand, Central Bank of Nigeria Governor Olayemi Cardoso discusses Nigeria’s economic recovery reforms to attract long-term investment and the monetary policy implications of easing inflation. He also examines financial ties with China and other Asian economies, banking recapitalisation, monetary policy committee independence, and the promise and perils of artificial intelligence. Nume Ekeghe presents excerpts A s the meetings have come to an end, can you speak to the key takeaways from the IMF/World Bank Annual Meetings? These meetings come at an important moment for Nigeria. Our economic indicators are showing encouraging improvements following a period of significant policy adjustments. Headline inflation declined to 14.63 per cent in September 2026, a development we welcomed during our stay here, from approximately 34.8 per cent at the end of 2024. External reserves have risen above $55 billion, their highest level in 18 years, while formal remittance inflows are approaching $1 billion monthly. Our banking sector has mobilised N4.65 trillion in fresh capital through the recapitalisation exercise, with just over 70 per cent raised from domestic investors. This strong domestic participation is an important vote of confidence in the resilience and long-term prospects of Nigeria’s banking system. International assessments are also recognising the progress being made, including Fitch’s recent revision of Nigeria’s sovereign outlook from stable to positive. These are important gains, and I want to acknowledge the patience and resilience of the Nigerian people through a difficult period of adjustment. Our responsibility now is to consolidate this progress and translate it into greater investment, productivity and sustainable economic growth. During the Annual Meetings, we engaged international partners on Nigeria’s economic outlook and the challenges facing the global economy. Together with the Honourable Minister of Finance and Coordinating Minister of the Economy, we held bilateral meetings with the IMF’s First Deputy Managing Director, Mr Dan Katz, and the President of the World Bank Group, Mr Ajay Banga. Our discussions covered Nigeria’s policy priorities and opportunities for continued cooperation. We also engaged S&P Global Ratings, Moody’s and Fitch Ratings on Nigeria’s economic outlook and the path towards attaining investment-grade ratings in the medium term. These engagements underscored the importance of policy consistency, credible institutions and effective coordination between fiscal and monetary authorities. Beyond these bilateral engagements, I was pleased to contribute Nigeria’s experience to two important IMF discussions. At the flagship debate on the global economy, Forging Resilient and Dynamic Economies for the Future, I joined IMF Managing Director Kristalina Georgieva and other international leaders to share lessons from Nigeria’s experience for emerging economies. For Africa, the ambition must go beyond surviving crises. In an increasingly uncertain global economy, we must build economies that are more productive, competitive and resilient to future shocks. We also discussed artificial intelligence and its implications for the global economy. Africa cannot afford to be a passive participant in the AI revolution. We must develop the capabilities to shape its opportunities and capture the economic value it creates. At the IMF seminar titled How Central Banks Decide: Committee Decision-Making and Communication in an Era of Uncertainty, moderated by Mr Dan Katz, I discussed the CBN’s experience in strengthening monetary policy decision-making, transparency and communication. Our September decision to reset the monetary policy rate from 26.5 per cent to 23 per cent provided a practical example of the importance of credible institutions and clear policy communication.",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ng"
   ],
