   "_dt": 1792127683.0,
   "ageDays": 3.17,
   "summary": "In 2024, Nigeria imported N14.15 trillion worth of goods from China while exporting only N2.99 trillion, imports accounting for over 82 percent of bilateral trade. Nigeria’s exports to China are concentrated in oil, gas, sesame, and raw ore; its imports from China are synthetic textiles, telephones, and machinery, and electrical equipment, higher-value manufactured goods. Land administration in Nigeria; challenges and way forward",
   "summary_zh": "2024年，尼日利亚从中国进口了价值N14.15万亿的商品，而出口仅为N2.99万亿，进口占双边贸易的82%以上。尼日利亚对中国的出口集中在石油、天然气、芝麻和原矿石；从中国的进口是合成纺织品、电话、机械和电气设备等高价值制成品。尼日利亚的土地管理；挑战与前进方向",
   "summarySource": "index",
   "body": "The trade structure is damning. In 2024, Nigeria imported N14.15 trillion worth of goods from China while exporting only N2.99 trillion, imports accounting for over 82 percent of bilateral trade. Nigeria’s exports to China are concentrated in oil, gas, sesame, and raw ore; its imports from China are synthetic textiles, telephones, and machinery, and electrical equipment, higher-value manufactured goods. This structure fits dependency theory’s “centre-periphery” relationship exactly: the periphery supplies raw materials, the centre supplies finished goods, and value flows systematically from periphery to centre. Land administration in Nigeria; challenges and way forward Has Nigeria’s indigenous industrialisation policy failed?",
